//! Send one HTTP request, follow redirects, decode the content, and deliver the body to a
//! destination that you choose.
//!
//! # When to use
//! Use this module when you need more control than `silk.http.client`: a sink for a large body, a
//! body that you stream, content decoding, a proxy, or a connection pool. Make a [`Fetch`] around a
//! [`FetchClient`], such as the `Context` of `silk.http.fetch_native`. Then call one function for
//! each request.
//!
//! # Details
//! There are three destinations:
//! - `toSink*` gives the body to a [`Sink`] in chunks, in order.
//! - `collect*` reads the whole body into memory, up to `maxBodyBytes`, and returns it only if it
//!   is complete.
//! - `discard*` reads and checks the body, and keeps nothing.
//!
//! The suffix tells what the request body is:
//! - `Empty`: no body.
//! - `Bytes`: a byte slice. It is sent again after a redirect that keeps the body.
//! - `OneShot`: a `BodyProducer` that can run only one time. A redirect that must send the body
//!   again fails with `ReplayUnavailable`.
//! - `Replay`: a factory that makes a new producer for each attempt.
//!
//! [`FetchOptions`] sets redirects, content decoding, the pool, the proxy, one deadline for the
//! whole operation, and all limits. `silk.http.redirect` applies the redirect rules, and
//! `silk.http.content` decodes the content.
//!
//! # Errors
//! - [`FetchError`] tells the phase and the reason, with the status and the URI when they are
//!   known, and how many body bytes the destination got.
//! - `RedirectError`, `ExchangeError`, `ValueError`, and `BodyError` from the lower modules.
//! - [`DestinationFailure`] when your [`Sink`] fails.
//! - `OutOfMemoryError` if the `Allocator` fails.
//!
//! # Gotchas
//! The deadline is not renewed between redirects or phases. The default [`FetchOptions`] do not
//! follow redirects and do not decode content. Set `redirect` and `content` to change that.
//!
//! @category HTTP

